This service downloads third-party engines and models on demand when
MaiPai Home asks it to (llama.cpp, MLX-based servers, ComfyUI, speech
runtimes, and the models the household selects). None of them are
vendored here. Each is fetched from its pinned upstream URL, checksum
verified, and used under its own licence, which the Stack records with
the download and reports before selection. Attributions for anything this
repo redistributes are listed here as they are added.

- hono (MIT) - https://hono.dev (the backend HTTP framework)
- @hono/zod-openapi (MIT) - https://github.com/honojs/middleware (OpenAPI schemas for the backend routes)
- @scalar/hono-api-reference (MIT) - https://github.com/scalar/scalar (the in-browser API reference for the backend)
- zod (MIT) - https://zod.dev (schema validation for the backend routes)
- drizzle-orm (Apache-2.0) - https://orm.drizzle.team (the backend database layer)
- drizzle-kit (MIT) - https://orm.drizzle.team (generating the backend database schema)
- typescript (Apache-2.0) - https://www.typescriptlang.org/ (the backend language)
- @types/bun (MIT) - https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped/tree/master/types/bun (type definitions for the backend)
- @huggingface/gguf (MIT) - https://github.com/huggingface/huggingface.js (GGUF header and remote range parser)
- @huggingface/hub (MIT) - https://github.com/huggingface/huggingface.js (model metadata and file lists for provenance)
- gguf-parser-go (MIT) - https://github.com/gpustack/gguf-parser-go; Copyright (c) 2024 gguf-parser-go authors

The Stack downloads a pinned release at run time and does not vendor it.
- ajv (MIT) - https://ajv.js.org (validating the wire-shape fixtures against their JSON Schemas in the test suite)
- ajv-formats (MIT) - https://github.com/ajv-validator/ajv-formats (the date-time format for those schemas)
- Parakeet TDT 0.6B v2 (NVIDIA, CC-BY-4.0) - https://huggingface.co/nvidia/parakeet-tdt-0.6b-v2 (the speech-to-text model for the 64 GB and 128 GB tiers, converted for sherpa-onnx by k2-fsa; downloaded at run time, not vendored)
- pyannote segmentation 3.0 (MIT) - https://huggingface.co/pyannote/segmentation-3.0 (speaker segmentation, int8 ONNX export by k2-fsa sherpa-onnx; downloaded at run time, not vendored)
- NVIDIA NeMo TitaNet small, nemo_en_titanet_small (CC-BY-4.0) - https://catalog.ngc.nvidia.com/orgs/nvidia/teams/nemo/models/titanet_small (speaker embedding for speaker detection, ONNX export by k2-fsa sherpa-onnx; Copyright NVIDIA Corporation; downloaded at run time, not vendored)
- LivePortrait (KwaiVGI, MIT) - https://github.com/KwaiVGI/LivePortrait, commit 9b294b3d0536135442ea73cb01e6cb3ca7029dd3 (still mouth and eye frames for talking companions; the source archive is downloaded at run time by sha256 and patched when the environment is built, not vendored). Its six model files (landmark.onnx, appearance_feature_extractor.pth, motion_extractor.pth, spade_generator.pth, warping_module.pth, stitching_retargeting_module.pth) come from the Hugging Face repository KwaiVGI/LivePortrait at revision 82a4fa6735ca58432b6ce39301b4b9ee066dea47, whose model card declares the licence MIT for the repository; each is pinned by sha256 and downloaded at run time. LivePortrait's own licence file says the face finder it ships (research-only models) must be replaced for commercial use: the Stack never downloads or uses it.
- Apple Vision (a system framework, no files shipped) - face rectangles for talking-picture frames through a helper the Stack compiles on the Mac; MediaPipe (Apache-2.0) - https://github.com/google-ai-edge/mediapipe (the fallback face detector, installed by pinned wheel at run time, not vendored)
